description.en:
Tells the engine the outcome of the duel or shot it asked a script to resolve.
In this game the clash maths runs in Squirrel. When a clash happens, the engine calls a ScBtlCalc_* function: the shared ones are in ie4_soccer_sys.nut, and a match script can replace them. That function computes both sides' values, decides the outcome and reports it here.
The callbacks in ie4_soccer_sys.nut that call it:
- ScBtlCalc_CMDBTL (1-on-1 duel): 4 arguments;
- ScBtlCalc_CMDSHOOT (shot): 5 arguments, with the miss flag;
- ScBtlCalc_CMDKEEPER (goalkeeper save): 1 argument;
- ScBtlCalc_CMDCHAIN (chain shot): 3 arguments;
- ScBtlCalc_CMDBLOCK (shot block): 3 arguments;
- ScBtlCalc_CMDKESHIN (Fighting Spirit clash): 4 arguments, where the two values are the damage dealt to each side;
- ScBtlCalc_CMD_TACTICS (tactic clash): 1 argument.
The parameter names below are the developers' local variable names in ie4_soccer_sys.nut.

Arguments are read in groups: the two values only when at least 3 arguments are given, the foul flag from 4 arguments, the miss flag from 5.

examples.code:
// No defending value here; the fifth argument says whether it missed
local miss = IsMiss_HeadVolley(_id, _bAvt);
CMND_BTL_SET_RESULT(true, atk_power, 0, false, miss);

// Replacement of the duel callback: the attacker always wins, with
// random values.
ScBtlCalc_CMDBTL <- function ( _cmdAtk, _cmdDef, _avtAtk, _avtDef )
{
	local trg = CMND_BTL_GET_RAND(50, 99);
	local tnm = CMND_BTL_GET_RAND(1, 19);
	CMND_BTL_SET_RESULT(true, trg, tnm, false);
};
